import { ailSource } from '../../lib/ail';
---

<AilPage lang="en">
  <p>
    Every post on this blog shows its AI Influence Level at the top. The scale comes from <a href={ailSource}>Daniel Miessler</a>,
    and this site uses its version 2.0 with one reading of my own, explained below. It says how much
    of a text an AI made, and who had the idea and started it. It says how a text was made, not whether it is any
    good.
  </p>
  <h2>How I use it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Every post gets a level</strong>, decided per post.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>My research posts are written with my personal AI setup</strong>, mostly Claude. They are at 3 where
      the structure of the finished text is mine, and at 4 where the AI gave my idea its structure and that
      structure stayed. The reading of my own: level 3 also covers a structure I set afterwards, by reordering and
      regrouping a draft in depth, because what counts is the finished text, not who started it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The level does not change how a post is checked.</strong> Every fact has a source, quotations are
      taken from the original documents, and I read and check every post before it goes online.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h2>How to read the badge</h2>
  <p>
    The badge shows the level as a number and as a bar of six segments, filled up to the level. The colour runs
    from green at 0 to brick red at 6 without judging either end: a high level describes how a text was made, not
    that it is worse.
  </p>
  <h2>The seven levels</h2>
</AilPage>
